me:
>>I would much rather have it treat pools and monsters as walls when moving
>>non-aggresively, i.e. no message and no wasted turn.  After all, a monster
>>blocking your way is a lot like a wall.  As long as it's there, you can't
>>walk that way.  Same with a pool.  You can attack, or swim, but not walk.

steve:
>Well, if you mis-key by attempting to walk into a pool or a monster, you
>have made a mistake.  I personally don't think that this should be a fatal
>mistake (unless the monster is really tough and you are not).  But it *is*
>a mistake.

Then, do you think it should cost you a turn to attempt to walk into a wall
or a closed door?  How about attempting to move into solid rock, such as
off of a corridor?  How about if you start to cast a spell, then change
your mind by hitting escape?

Attempting to walk into water when (in the proposed new arrangement)
one can only #swim in it, or attempting to walk through a monster is
a mistake, yes.  But it is a typing mistake.  I thought the whole point
of this discussion was to avoid penalizing players for typos, while
still giving them what they deserve for "thinkos" (got that from the
hacker jargon dictionary!), and not giving anything away.

BTW I feel that when the player is confused, commands should be obeyed
unconditionally.  Typing 'h' should cause an attempt to move, and if this
turns out to be to the right, into some water, so be it.  It should cost
a turn even if the player ends up walking into a wall.  A scam I've been
using is to stand in a corridor when confused, so that I will always hit
my attacker or retreat.  A confused character should not be able to pull
this off, IMHO.

